1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,666
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,666 --> 00:00:07,384
This is chapter seventeen: choosing the right collection.

3
00:00:07,834 --> 00:00:12,465
Most Java code uses ArrayList, HashMap, and occasionally HashSet.

4
00:00:12,465 --> 00:00:16,313
That's usually right, which is why the habit persists.

5
00:00:16,313 --> 00:00:25,647
And it's why the cases where it's wrong go unnoticed, until something is slow, iterates in a surprising order, or throws on a null.

6
00:00:26,547 --> 00:00:28,077
Here are four defects.

7
00:00:28,077 --> 00:00:34,543
A membership test on a forty-thousand-element list, inside a loop, so it's quadratic overall.

8
00:00:34,543 --> 00:00:38,715
A HashMap exported to CSV, whose order changes between runs.

9
00:00:38,715 --> 00:00:41,844
A lookup of null on Map dot of, which throws.

10
00:00:41,844 --> 00:00:46,572
And indexed access on a LinkedList, which walks the list every time.

11
00:00:46,572 --> 00:00:48,241
None of these is subtle.

12
00:00:48,241 --> 00:00:50,953
Each is just one question nobody asked.

13
00:00:51,853 --> 00:00:53,973
Ask four questions, in order.

14
00:00:53,973 --> 00:00:56,824
First: key-value pairs, or just values?

15
00:00:56,824 --> 00:00:59,383
That decides Map versus Collection.

16
00:00:59,383 --> 00:01:04,354
Map isn't a Collection at all, because it holds pairs, not elements.

17
00:01:04,804 --> 00:01:06,931
Second: are duplicates allowed?

18
00:01:06,931 --> 00:01:08,921
That decides List versus Set.

19
00:01:09,371 --> 00:01:11,308
Third: what order do you need?

20
00:01:11,308 --> 00:01:14,214
Insertion order, sorted order, or don't care.

21
00:01:14,664 --> 00:01:17,348
Fourth: will it be accessed concurrently?

22
00:01:17,348 --> 00:01:19,443
Plain, concurrent, or immutable.

23
00:01:19,893 --> 00:01:24,163
Answer those and the implementation is almost always decided.

24
00:01:24,163 --> 00:01:31,583
Most wrong choices come from skipping question three, and mistaking unspecified order for insertion order.

25
00:01:32,483 --> 00:01:34,876
Here's the cheat sheet by intent.

26
00:01:34,876 --> 00:01:37,921
A sequence with indexed access: ArrayList.

27
00:01:37,921 --> 00:01:40,531
A queue, stack or deque: ArrayDeque.

28
00:01:40,531 --> 00:01:48,144
For uniqueness, HashSet when order doesn't matter, LinkedHashSet for insertion order, TreeSet for sorted.

29
00:01:48,144 --> 00:01:51,116
The same three-way split applies to maps.

30
00:01:51,116 --> 00:01:53,582
EnumMap and EnumSet for enum keys.

31
00:01:53,582 --> 00:01:58,367
PriorityQueue when you always want the highest priority item next.

32
00:01:58,367 --> 00:02:01,484
ConcurrentHashMap for a shared mutable map.

33
00:02:01,484 --> 00:02:06,632
And List dot of, Set dot of and Map dot of for small fixed collections.

34
00:02:07,532 --> 00:02:09,752
Two tables people actually need.

35
00:02:09,752 --> 00:02:14,956
Nulls: TreeSet and TreeMap keys reject null, because they must be compared.

36
00:02:14,956 --> 00:02:16,482
ArrayDeque rejects it.

37
00:02:16,482 --> 00:02:24,322
ConcurrentHashMap rejects null keys and values, so swapping it in for a HashMap can break code that stored nulls.

38
00:02:24,322 --> 00:02:32,023
And the immutable factories reject nulls so strictly that even contains null throws instead of returning false.

39
00:02:32,473 --> 00:02:38,310
Order: HashMap and HashSet order is unspecified, and changes as the map grows.

40
00:02:38,310 --> 00:02:43,399
Set dot of and Map dot of randomise it on every JVM run, on purpose.

41
00:02:43,399 --> 00:02:46,093
And PriorityQueue is the sneaky one.

42
00:02:46,093 --> 00:02:49,536
It's a heap, so iterating it gives heap order.

43
00:02:49,536 --> 00:02:52,679
Only repeated poll gives you sorted order.

44
00:02:52,679 --> 00:02:55,598
That often looks right on small inputs.

45
00:02:56,498 --> 00:02:57,200
Two fixes.

46
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:01,974
If a collection exists only to answer, is this in it, make it a Set.

47
00:03:01,974 --> 00:03:08,082
Change one word, and a forty-thousand-element scan per cart line becomes a hash lookup.

48
00:03:08,532 --> 00:03:13,273
And choose TreeMap for range queries, not just sorted iteration.

49
00:03:13,273 --> 00:03:15,866
Every slot between nine and twelve.

50
00:03:15,866 --> 00:03:18,682
The next slot at or after ten fifteen.

51
00:03:18,682 --> 00:03:20,756
The last one before closing.

52
00:03:20,756 --> 00:03:25,497
A HashMap can't answer any of those without scanning everything.

53
00:03:25,497 --> 00:03:28,905
The log n cost buys a whole category of query.

54
00:03:29,805 --> 00:03:35,503
For enum keys, EnumMap and EnumSet are strictly better than the hash versions.

55
00:03:35,503 --> 00:03:39,593
EnumMap is an array indexed by ordinal, with no hashing.

56
00:03:39,593 --> 00:03:47,409
EnumSet is a bitset, so a set of up to sixty-four constants is a single long, and contains is one bit test.

57
00:03:47,409 --> 00:03:50,477
They're underused only because habit wins.

58
00:03:50,927 --> 00:03:54,591
And know the difference between a copy and a view.

59
00:03:54,591 --> 00:03:57,156
List dot copyOf is a real snapshot.

60
00:03:57,156 --> 00:04:06,317
Collections dot unmodifiableList is a read-only view, so the caller can't write, but sees every later change to the original.

61
00:04:06,317 --> 00:04:08,442
For accessors, return a copy.

62
00:04:09,342 --> 00:04:12,903
Four collections never to use in new code.

63
00:04:12,903 --> 00:04:16,294
Vector synchronises every single method.

64
00:04:16,294 --> 00:04:17,904
It's from Java 1.0.

65
00:04:17,904 --> 00:04:19,091
Use ArrayList.

66
00:04:19,541 --> 00:04:23,378
Hashtable has the same problem, and rejects nulls too.

67
00:04:23,378 --> 00:04:26,859
Use HashMap, or ConcurrentHashMap if it's shared.

68
00:04:27,309 --> 00:04:32,619
Stack extends Vector, and iterates from the bottom up, the opposite of pop order.

69
00:04:32,619 --> 00:04:33,602
Use ArrayDeque.

70
00:04:34,052 --> 00:04:40,173
And LinkedList is almost always slower than ArrayList, because of poor cache locality.

71
00:04:40,173 --> 00:04:42,095
The next chapter shows why.

72
00:04:42,995 --> 00:04:44,111
A few internals.

73
00:04:44,111 --> 00:04:47,391
Declare fields as interfaces, like List or Map.

74
00:04:47,391 --> 00:04:56,883
Then you can switch to LinkedHashMap when order starts to matter, or ConcurrentHashMap when it becomes shared, without touching callers.

75
00:04:57,333 --> 00:05:03,208
ArrayList implements a marker interface called RandomAccess, and LinkedList doesn't.

76
00:05:03,208 --> 00:05:08,383
Generic code that index-loops over any List is quadratic on a linked list.

77
00:05:08,833 --> 00:05:14,585
Fail-fast iterators throw ConcurrentModificationException on a best-effort basis.

78
00:05:14,585 --> 00:05:18,348
It's a bug detector, not a synchronisation mechanism.

79
00:05:18,798 --> 00:05:19,708
And sizing.

80
00:05:19,708 --> 00:05:26,821
new HashMap of n takes a capacity, not a count, so it resizes at seventy-five percent.

81
00:05:26,821 --> 00:05:32,281
Java 19's HashMap dot newHashMap of n does the arithmetic for you.

82
00:05:33,181 --> 00:05:34,439
Production rules.

83
00:05:34,439 --> 00:05:36,142
Ask the four questions.

84
00:05:36,142 --> 00:05:44,212
Use a Set for membership tests, TreeMap and TreeSet for range queries, and EnumMap and EnumSet for enum keys.

85
00:05:44,212 --> 00:05:46,359
Return copies from accessors.

86
00:05:46,359 --> 00:05:49,394
And use ArrayDeque for stacks and queues.

87
00:05:49,844 --> 00:05:55,811
Never rely on HashMap or HashSet order, or on Set dot of and Map dot of order.

88
00:05:55,811 --> 00:05:58,870
Never call contains null on List dot of.

89
00:05:58,870 --> 00:06:03,001
Never iterate a PriorityQueue expecting sorted output.

90
00:06:03,001 --> 00:06:05,831
Never use Vector, Hashtable or Stack.

91
00:06:05,831 --> 00:06:09,044
And don't reach for LinkedList by default.

92
00:06:09,944 --> 00:06:10,610
To recap.

93
00:06:10,610 --> 00:06:15,498
Ask four questions: key-value, duplicates, order, and concurrency.

94
00:06:15,498 --> 00:06:20,829
Use a Set for membership, TreeMap for ranges, and EnumMap for enum keys.

95
00:06:20,829 --> 00:06:27,864
Remember that unspecified order is not insertion order, and know which collections reject null.

96
00:06:28,314 --> 00:06:34,224
Next is chapter eighteen, list internals, where we look inside ArrayList and LinkedList.

97
00:06:34,224 --> 00:06:35,567
Thanks for watching.
